📡 Multi-SIM Streaming Visualiser
A browser-based visualiser for exploring multi-SIM bonded streaming. It demonstrates how a live video feed can be split across multiple cellular modems (SIM cards) and reassembled for Twitch, allowing you to simulate SIM outages in real-time. You can compare two transmission strategies: Variant A (splitting the frame into quadrants) and Variant B (pixel round-robin, where every SIM sends the full frame but only 1/n of the pixels survive).

⚠️ What this is not
❌ Not a real encoder or transport. Nothing is compressed, packetised, or sent over a network.

❌ Not a network model. It does not simulate latency, jitter, variable bitrate, or per-link throttling — only binary up/down.

❌ Not a claim that one variant is strictly better. Variant A is cheaper to encode; Variant B degrades more gracefully. The right choice depends on your hardware budget, your encoder, and how much you care about a viewer noticing a dropout.

🧪 Ideas for extending it
□ Add per-SIM bandwidth limits and show the assembler struggling to keep up.
□ Introduce transient failures (a SIM drops for 200 ms then returns) and show recovery.
□ Replace the nearest-neighbour fill with a real edge-aware inpainting pass.
□ Add a third variant: slice-based (horizontal stripes) — the common middle ground.
□ Animate the ownership map reshuffling when *n* changes, so you can see pixels migrate.
□ Log a fake "viewer-perceived quality" score per variant per outage.